Here we have used the standard names 1T* and 1NIL* to represent true and
false.  (Traditionally 1NIL* is also considered to be the empty list,
but we will avoid this here, writing "1()*" for the empty list.)
	Because LISP programs are represented as LISP data structures
(S-expressions), there is a difficulty with representing constants.
For example, suppose we want to determine whether or not the value
of the variable 1X* is the atomic symbol "1FOO*".
We might try writing:
.sp
.nofill
.spw 13
.block 1
