Because the 1QUOTE* construction is used so frequently in LISP,
we use an abbreviated notation: "1'FOO*" is equivalent to "1(QUOTE FOO)*".
This is only a notational convenience; the two notations denote
the same S-expression.  (S-expressions are not character strings,
but data objects with a certain structure.  We use character strings to notate
S-expressions on paper, but we can use other notations as well,
such as little boxes and arrows.  We can and do allow several different character strings
to denote the same S-expression.)

