.sp
	1DRIVER-LOOP* reads an S-expression from the input stream
and passes it, along with the current procedure definitions,
to 1DRIVER-LOOP-1*.  This procedure in turn determines whether the input
S-expression is a definition.  If it is, then it uses 1BIND*
(described below) to produce an augmented set of procedure definitions,
prints the name of the defined procedure, and calls 1DRIVER-LOOP*
to repeat the process.  The augmented set of procedures is passed to
1DRIVER-LOOP*, and so the variable 1PROCEDURES* always
contains all the accumulated definitions ever read.
If the input S-expression is not a definition, then it is given
to the evaluator 1EVAL*, whose purpose is to determine
the values of expressions.  {Note Value Quibble}
The set of currently defined procedures
is also passed to 1EVAL*.
	The process carried on by the driver loop is often called the "top level";
all user programs and requests are run "under" it.  The growing set
