.sp
	The interpreter uses several utility procedures
for maintaining symbol tables (see Figure 3).
A symbol table is represented as a list of buckets;
each bucket is a list whose car is a list of names
and whose cdr is a list of corresponding values.
{Note This ain't A-lists}
If a variable name occurs in more than one bucket,
the leftmost such bucket has priority; in this way
new symbol definitions added to the front of the list
can supersede old ones.
	1BIND* takes a list of names, a list of values,
and a symbol table, and produces a new symbol table which is the old
one augmented by an extra bucket containing the new set of
associations.  (It also performs a useful error check
4~0 1LENGTH* returns the length of a list.)
	1VALUE* is essentially an interface to 1LOOKUP*.
We define it because later, in Part Three, we will want to use different versions
of 1VALUE1* without changing the underlying algorithm in 1LOOKUP*.
The check for 1&UNBOUND* catches incorrect references to undefined variables.
	1LOOKUP* takes a name and a symbol table,
and returns that portion of a bucket whose car is the
associated value.  (This definition will be more useful later than one
in which the value itself is returned.)
.sp
	Note carefully the use of the variable 1PROCEDURES*
in the interpreter.
When 1DRIVER-LOOP-1* calls 1EVAL* it passes
the current list of defined procedures (both primitive and user-defined).
1DRIVER-LOOP-1* is the only routine which augments the value of
1PROCEDURES*, and this value is only used in 1EVAL*, when it
is passed to 1VALUE*.
However, all of the routines 1APPLY*, 1EVCOND*, and
1EVLIS* have to know about 1PROCEDURES*, and dutifully pass
it along so that it may be eventually used by 1EVAL*.
The set of definitions must be passed along because there is
no provision for free variables or side effects; there is no
way to have "memory" or "state" other than in passed variables.
The absence of free variables effectively causes our language
to be referentially transparent.  However, we sense
a disturbing lack of modularity in the use of 1PROCEDURES*
(and, to a lesser extent, in the use of 1ENV* 4~* look at
1EVCOND* and 1EVLIS*).  We will return to this point later.
	Our recursion equations language has no special
iteration or looping constructs, such as the Algol for
statement or the FORTRAN DO loop.  All loops are constructed by
arranging for recursive procedures to call themselves or each other.
For example, 1EVCOND* (see Figure 2) iterates over the clauses of a 1COND*
by calling itself on successive "tails" of the list of clauses.
Now such recursive calls may strike the reader familiar with
other languages (such as Algol, FORTRAN, PL/I, etc.)
on an intuitive level as being rather inefficient
for implementing real programs.
Even granted that calls might be made fast, they would
seem to consume space in the form of return addresses
and other control information.
Examination of the recursion equations evaluator
will show, however, that this phenomenon does not have to occur.
This is because no extra information is saved if there is nothing left
to do on return from a recursive call.
See [SCHEME] and [Debunking] for a more thorough discussion of this.



