.section
Decomposition of State
.sp
	We saw in Part One that an interactive top-level loop
necessarily violates referential transparency.
We wish to deal with the computer
as an entity with state, which changes over time by interacting
with a user.  In particular, we want the computer to change over time by accumulating
procedure definitions.
	Just as the user wishes to think of the computer
as having state, he may find it conceptually convenient
to organize a program similarly:  one part may deal with another part
having state.
Often programs are written for the purpose of analyzing or simulating
a physical system.  If modules of the program are to reflect
the conceptual divisions of the physical system, then the program
modules may well need to have independent state variables.
Thus the notion of state is not just a programming trick,
but may be required by the nature of the problem domain.
	A simpler example of the use of state involves the
use of a pseudo-random number generator.
A LISP version of one might be:
.sp
.nofill
.spw 13
.block 5
1     (DEFINE (RANDOM SEED)
	     ((LAMBDA (Z)
		      (COND ((> Z 0) Z)
			    (T (+ Z -32768.))))
	      (* SEED 899.)))
.spw 16
.adjust
