but as a side effect alters 1X* so that its car or cdr,
respectively, is the value of 1Y*.  (The introduction
of operators which modify S-expressions causes a number
of nasty problems, which we will consider presently.)
We will use these operators to alter the structure of the environment
1ENV*.  We modify 1EVAL* to recognize the 1SETQ* construct
(see Figure 11).
On seeing "1SETQ*" in the "operator position" of the expression,
1EVAL* dispatches to 1EVSETQ*, after recursively evaluating
the value to be assigned.  1EVSETQ* uses 1LOOKUP* to find
the effective binding of the variable mentioned in the 1SETQ*.
If there is such a binding, 1RPLACA* is used to change the value
associated with the variable.  If there is no such binding,
then the intent is to initialize a top-level variable;
1EV-TOP-LEVEL-SETQ* locates the top-level environment
(which is always at the end of any environment) and creates
a new binding by altering the environment structure.
	We also modify 1EVAL* to recognize 1PROGN*.
1EVPROGN* is a tail-recursive loop which evaluates
each subform of the 1PROGN* form in turn,
throwing away each value but the last.
