.sp
	Although this is the tradition, it doesn't work very well.
The problem is that the lexical variables are not really lexical.
Although lexical references cannot incorrectly refer to dynamically
intended bindings, the reverse is not true.
Dynamic variable references can be captured by bindings
intended to be strictly lexical.
	For example, we might want to write a procedure which packages
up information about dealing with 1RADIX*:
.sp
.nofill
.spw 13
.block 3
1     (DEFINE (RADIX-10 FUN)
	     ((LAMBDA (RADIX) (FUN))
	      10.))
.spw 16
.adjust
.sp
0
This is more general than 1PRINT-10* in that it allows us to wrap
a binding of 1RADIX* around any piece of code, not just a call
to 1PRINT*.  (In a more realistic example, we might package up the bindings
of a dozen parameters in a similar manner.)
	There are two possibilities: should the argument to 1RADIX-10*
be a closed procedure or an open 1LAMBDA*-expression?
If closed:
.sp
.nofill
.spw 13
.block 2
1     (DEFINE (DO-SOMETHING-INTERESTING X FUN)
	     (RADIX-10 (LAMBDA () (FORMAT-HAIR 'FOO (CADR X) FUN))))
.spw 16
.adjust
.sp
0
(1FORMAT-HAIR* takes several arguments,
one of them a procedure and presumably calls 1PRINT* at some level),
then the binding of 1RADIX*
in 1RADIX-10* will not be apparent to 1PRINT*, because the
environment of the call to 1FORMAT-HAIR* is that of the closed
procedure, which in turn is that of the call to 1RADIX-10*
within 1DO-SOMETHING-INTERESTING*.  Thus it fails to work at all.
If the argument to 1RADIX-10* is left open:
.sp
.nofill
.spw 13
.block 2
1     (DEFINE (DO-SOMETHING-INTERESTING X FUN)
	     (RADIX-10 '(LAMBDA () (FORMAT-HAIR 'FOO (CADR X) FUN))))
.spw 16
.adjust
.sp
0
then this fails to work at all because of a variable naming conflict
with 1FUN*.  The third argument passed to 1FORMAT-HAIR* will
evaluate to the argument which was passed to 1RADIX-10*, namely the
quoted lambda expression.  This is similar to the
1MAPCAR* bug that originally got us thinking about lexical scoping in Part One.
	A solution to this problem is to maintain separate environments
for lexical and dynamic variables; this will guarantee that the
two kinds cannot interfere with each other.
This will require a special syntax for distinguishing references to
and bindings of the two kinds of variables.
We will choose to encode lexical variables as atomic symbols, as before,
and dynamic variables as lists of the form 1(DYNAMIC_x)*, where 1x*
is the name of the dynamic variable.
(This choice is completely arbitrary.  We could have chosen
to encode the two kinds as 1(LEXICAL_x)* and 1x*;
or as 1(LEXICAL_x)* and 1(DYNAMIC_x)*, leaving atomic symbols
as such free to encode yet something else;
but we have chosen this because in practice most variable references,
even in a purely dynamically scoped LISP,
are lexical, or can be considered so.)
	In our new interpreter (see Figure 15) we call the two environments
1ENV* (lexical) and 1DENV* (dynamic).
The syntax of 1LAMBDA*-expressions is extended to accommodate two kinds
of bindings; for example,
.sp
.nofill
.spw 13
.block 1
1     (LAMBDA (X Y (DYNAMIC Z) W) ...)
.spw 16
.adjust
