.spreadnote
/{1QUOTE* Shafts the Compiler}//Page 19/
.sp
	We emphasize that it is not the presence of dynamically
scoped variables which makes standard LISP difficult for compilers,
but the very fact that the 1LAMBDA*-expressions are quoted.
It is impossible in general to determine whether a quoted
S-expression is intended to be code or just some constant data.
Most LISP systems provide another kind of 1QUOTE* called
1FUNCTION*.  In LISP 1 [LISP_1M] and LISP 1.5 [LISP_1.5M] this used to produce
1FUNARG* objects (we call them 1&PROCEDURE* objects),
but in more recent LISP systems [Moon] [Teitelman] an ordinary
1FUNCTION*-expression has been made equivalent to a quoted
expression, serving only as a flag to the compiler that the
quoted expression is intended as code.
However, the introduction of the "'" notation for quoted
expressions has led many programmers to prefer the use
of 1QUOTE* to 1FUNCTION* for reasons of conciseness.
This in turn has required changes to the compiler to specially
recognize standard situations where this is used (e.g. the
functional argument to 1MAPCAR*), but this patch doesn't
solve the problem generally.

