/{S-expression Postulates and Notation}//Pages 4, 41/
.sp
	S-expressions form a number system analogous to
that for the natural numbers.  Each can be used to encode
arbitrary strings of symbols by means of "G4o*delization",
but the S-expression encoding is usually far more convenient
than the numerical encoding.
	We repeat here the informal characterization
of Peano's postulates and the analogous postulates
for S-expressions from [Levin]:
.sp
.rtw 10
.center
The Postulates of Arithmetic
.br
1.  Zero is a number.
.br
2.  The successor of a number is a number.
.br
3.  Zero is not the successor of any number.
.br
4.  No two numbers have the same successor.
.br
5.  (Induction Principle)
Any property which is true for zero, and is such that if it is true
for some number it is also true for the successor of that number, it is
true for all numbers.
.rtw -10
.sp
	Zero is notated as 10*, and the successor of any number
1N* is notated as 1N'*.  As a convenience
we define alternative notations for numbers other than zero,
such as decimal place-value notation.  Thus for 10'''''''''''''*
we often write 113*.
.sp
.rtw 10
.center
The Postulates for S-expressions
.br
1.  Atoms are S-expressions.
.br
2.  The cons of any two S-expressions is an S-expression.
.br
3.  An atom is not the cons of any two S-expressions.
.br
4.  If  differs from , or if 	 differs from ,
then cons of  and 	 differs from cons of  and .
.br
5.  (Induction Principle)
Any property which is true of all atoms,
and is such that if it is true for two S-expressions
it is also true for their cons, is true for all S-expressions.
.rtw -10
.sp
	Atoms are notated as strings of letters and digits.
The cons of two S-expressions  and  is notated
1(0* . 0*)*.
As a convenience, we define alternative notations for some
commonly used forms of S-expression, such as list notation.
The atom 1NIL* is called the "empty list"; we write it as_1()*.
If 1(0__	*_..._0*)*
is (the notation for) a list 
(where the "1...*" is meant as a meta-syntactic ellipsis),
then the cons of  and  is written
1(0___	*_..._0*)*.
We also define quotation notation, in which
1(QUOTE 0*)* is written as 1'*.
	(This definition of S-expressions applies to "pure LISP",
which has no side effects.  In Part Two, when the 1RPLACA* and 1RPLACD*
operators are introduced, the phrase "the cons of" will not be
well-defined.)

