.sp
	To continue our 1GAUSSIAN* example (see {Note Gaussian}), we can
try to remove the side-effect from 1RANDOM* while avoiding
the passing around of 1SEED* by pushing 1RANDOM*
up to the top level (see Figure N10).
1RANDOM-DRIVER* takes a function 1F* and an initial seed
(reminiscent of 1<THE-PRIMITIVE-PROCEDURES>*), and
continually stuffs random numbers into 1F*.  Each call
to 1F* must produce a new 1F* (a kind of continuation [Reynolds]).
Using this, we can arrange for numbers with a "Gaussian"
distribution to be generated.
.sp
.block 22
.pscore -24
.spread
/ // /
.pscore 3
.nofill
.spw 13
.block 7
1     (DEFINE (RANDOM-DRIVER F SEED)
	     ((LAMBDA (NEWSEED)
		      (RANDOM-DRIVER (F NEWSEED) NEWSEED))
	      ((LAMBDA (Z)
		       (COND ((> Z 0) Z)
			     (T (+ Z -32768.))))
	       (* SEED 899.))))
.spw 16
.adjust
.sp
0
.nofill
.spw 13
.block 10
1     (DEFINE (GAUSSIAN G)
	     (WEBER 0 43 G))
.sp
     (DEFINE (WEBER X N H)
	     (COND ((= N 0) (H X))
		   (T (LAMBDA (R)
			      (WEBER (+ X R) (- N 1) H)))))
.sp
     (DEFINE (DRIVER USERFN INITSEED)
	     (RANDOM-DRIVER (GAUSSIAN USERFN) INITSEED))
.spw 16
.adjust
.sp
0
