.sp
In this way, a user function can be provided to 1DRIVER*
(along with the initial seed), and the user function will
have "Gaussian" numbers stuffed into it.
For example:
.sp
.nofill
.spw 13
.block 3
1     (DEFINE (P R) (PROGN (PRINT R) P))
.sp
     (DRIVER P 11)
.spw 16
.adjust
.sp
0
will print an interminable sequence of "Gaussian" numbers.
Notice the structure of the program:  the 1RANDOM*
procedure calls 1GAUSSIAN*, which in turn calls
the user procedure.  We have completely everted the overall system.
The more layers in the original system piled on top of
1GAUSSIAN*, the more layers will appear inside-out in this version.
	Now there are two other funny things about this.  One is that
we had to use a side effect (1PRINT*) to get the answer out;
the other is that it's hard to make it stop!  These problems
are related.  The structure of 1RANDOM-DRIVER* is an infinite loop,
as with all drivers.  Because 1RANDOM-DRIVER* never returns
a value, there is no way to get an answer out without a side-effect
like 1PRINT*.
	We can arrange to signal 1RANDOM-DRIVER* that no more values
are desired, and to return a value (see Figure N11).
.sp
.block 26
.pscore -24
.spread
/ // /
.pscore 3
.nofill
.spw 13
.block 8
1     (DEFINE (RANDOM-DRIVER F SEED)
	     (COND ((CAR F) (CDR F))
		   (T ((LAMBDA (NEWSEED)
			       (RANDOM-DRIVER ((CDR F) NEWSEED) NEWSEED))
		       ((LAMBDA (Z)
				(COND ((> Z 0) Z)
				      (T (+ Z -32768.))))
			(* SEED 899.))))))
.spw 16
.adjust
.sp
0
.nofill
.spw 13
.block 11
1     (DEFINE (GAUSSIAN G)
	     (WEBER 0 43 G))
.sp
     (DEFINE (WEBER X N H)
	     (COND ((= N 0) (H X))
		   (T (CONS NIL
			    (LAMBDA (R)
				    (WEBER (+ X R) (- N 1) H))))))
.sp
     (DEFINE (DRIVER USERFN)
	     (RANDOM-DRIVER (GAUSSIAN USERFN) 43))
.spw 16
.adjust
.sp
0
.center
Figure N11
.center
"Gaussian" Random-Number Generator "Top Level" without Side Effects
