/{Y-operator}//Pages 26, 57/
.sp
	While the interpreter of Figure 8 cannot 1DEFINE*
recursive procedures, it is possible to define recursive
procedures by using a variant of the "paradoxical combinator",
also known as the Y-operator:
.sp
.nofill
.spw 13
.block 7
1     (DEFINE (Y F)
	     ((LAMBDA (G)
		      (LAMBDA (X)
			      ((F (G G)) X)))
	      (LAMBDA (G)
		      (LAMBDA (X)
			      ((F (G G)) X)))))
.spw 16
.adjust
.sp
0
Using this we define the doubly-recursive
algorithm for computing the Fibonacci function:
.sp
.nofill
.spw 13
.block 7
1     (DEFINE (FIB K)
	     ((Y (LAMBDA (F)
			 (LAMBDA (N)
				 (COND ((= N 0) 1)
				       ((= N 1) 1)
				       (T (+ (F (- N 1)) (F (- N 2))))))))
	      K))
.spw 16
.adjust
.sp
0
That this manages to work is truly remarkable.
Notice that this is almost identical to the 1LABEL* construct
which was actually introduced by LISP 1, though at the time
it was invented the implementors didn't realize this correspondence
[LISP History].

